<title>INDIA: India parliament approves bill on depository trade.</title>
<headline>India parliament approves bill on depository trade.</headline>
<dateline>NEW DELHI 1997-03-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>India's parliament on Monday approved a bill allowing the securities of state-run banks and financial institutions to be traded through share depositories, the Press Trust of India said.</p>
<p>Earlier, a technical stipulation did not allow securities of the Industrial Development Bank of India, the Unit Trust of India, the State Bank of India and other state-run banks to be traded through the National Securities Depository Limited (NSDL).</p>
<p>Depositories allow the registration and transfer of shares by electronic means.</p>
<p>The bill approved last week by the Lok Sabha, the lower house of parliament, was cleared by the Rajya Sabha, the upper house, on Monday.</p>
<p>The bill replaces an ordinance issued by a presidential decree in January to make necessary changes to allow the securities to be traded through the NSDL.</p>
</text>
